Title:
Blending In: Why “Encrypt Everything” Makes You More Visible

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post argues that the common “keep shellcode fully encrypted in memory” approach increases detectability because it creates high-entropy, file-unbacked `MEM_PRIVATE` executable regions that stand out to modern EDR memory scanners. It explains what “legitimate” Windows process memory typically looks like (file-backed `MEM_IMAGE` mappings with moderate entropy and disk-matchable bytes) and frames evasion as mimicry rather than cryptography. The author describes an implant design (“Wraith” in the Lethe C2 project) that uses module stomping to place agent code inside the `.text` of an already-loaded DLL, preserving a normal-looking `MEM_IMAGE` region. To reduce exposure during idle time, a “warden” thread swaps the original DLL bytes back in before sleep and restores agent bytes on wake, minimizing the window where memory diverges from disk. It also covers indirect syscalls by jumping to a `syscall; ret` gadget inside `ntdll` so the syscall appears to originate from legitimate module code, with SSN resolution via Halo’s Gate and API name hashing to avoid plaintext strings. Useful for red teamers and implant developers focused on defeating memory-introspection and call-stack-based detections, and for defenders understanding what anomalies to hunt.

Technical Focus:
- EDR memory introspection signals (entropy, `MEM_PRIVATE` vs `MEM_IMAGE`, disk-to-memory mismatch)
- Module stomping of DLL `.text` sections
- Sleep masking via byte swapping and thread synchronization (NT events)
- Indirect syscalls using `ntdll` `syscall; ret` gadgets
- Syscall number discovery with Halo’s Gate
- API hashing (DJB2) and stringless resolution

Use Cases:
- Designing implants that avoid high-entropy “encrypted blob” memory signatures
- Implementing sleep-masking to reduce detection during beacon idle periods
- Building syscall paths that evade user-mode hooks while keeping plausible call stacks
- Creating red-team tradecraft to blend into normal Windows module memory layouts
- Developing defensive detections for module stomping and transient `.text` tampering

Keywords:
EDR evasion, memory scanning, entropy analysis, MEM_PRIVATE, MEM_IMAGE, SEC_IMAGE, module stomping, sleep masking, beacon sleep cycle, NtProtectVirtualMemory, NtMapViewOfSection, NtOpenSection, KnownDlls, indirect syscalls, syscall; ret gadget, ntdll, call stack spoofing, Halo’s Gate, SSN resolution, DJB2 hashing, pe-sieve, Moneta